            <ArticleTitle>Laparoscopic Sleeve-Collis-Nissen Gastroplasty: a Safe Alternative for Morbidly Obese Patients with Gastroesophageal Reflux Disease.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1217-22</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1007/s11695-014-1523-4</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Some studies have recently suggested that laparoscopic sleeve gastrectomy may exacerbate gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD) symptoms or even increase the risk of &quot;de novo&quot; post-operative GERD. We herein describe and evaluate the initial response of an alternative technique of sleeve gastroplasty combined with Nissen fundoplication for morbidly obese patients who present significant GERD.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">From January 2008 to December 2013, 122 morbidly obese patients underwent laparoscopic Sleeve-Collis-Nissen gastroplasty (LSCNG).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">The great majority of the patients were female (97.5 %), with a mean age of 42.4 years old (from 18 to 72). Hiatal hernia and use of proton pump inhibitors (PPIs) were presented in 54.9 and 92 %, respectively. The mean operative time was 91 ± 6 min. The mean hospitalization stay was 2 ± 0.3 days. Major complications including stenosis requiring endoscopic dilation and GI bleeding were observed in five patients (4.1 %). No leaks were observed. One-year follow-up showed a significant decrease in the prevalence of esophagitis (100 vs 13.6 %) and the use of PPIs (92 vs 13.6 %). The percentages of excess weight loss 1 and 3 years after the surgery were 64.4 ± 7.2 and 60.4 ± 8.1 %, respectively.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">LSCNG is a novel, technically feasible surgery with a low incidence of procedure-related complications. However, further prospective studies are required to assess the real impact of this procedure on the improvement of GERD symptoms.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
